From: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] cuda: cap the MMQ MoE J tile at 64 on sm_121

Port of the qwen3.6 gb10cuda series 0016 / laguna-xs gb10cuda series 0015
(verified on the trusted runner at +4.35% score / +2.76% score
respectively, prefill 1.0369 / 1.0741). For mul_mat_id,
mul_mat_q_switch_J picks J from ncols_max = n_tokens, landing at J=128
for prefill batches, while the 256-expert top-8 segments average ~16
columns at ub512. Laguna-S shares the top-8-of-256 routing of both
donors, so the qwen target sweep (J 16..128 peaked at 56-64; the
RDNA4-style near-segment-width J=32 loses 9%) transfers: sm_121
tensor-core MMQ amortizes per-tile fixed cost over wide column tiles, so
the padding-vs-tile-cost sweet spot sits at J=64, not at the segment
width. Cap the mmid path at the smallest valid config >= 64
(arch-guarded to sm_121; dense matmuls untouched; decode expert matvecs
route via mmvq, not MMQ). GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_MMQ_MOE_J restores stock
selection.

Measured (runner box gx10-838f, healthy clocks 2405-2554 MHz), same-binary
toggle A/B on top of series 0001, 3 interleaved rounds: pp512 608.7/610.3
off vs 636.7/635.0 on (rounds 1-2, per-round ratios 1.0460/1.0405); round
3 drew the documented per-launch fast decode mode on the OFF arm (tg64
25.90 vs 24.2-24.4 in every other launch) and read 627.8 off / 600.5 on -
the ~7% per-launch lottery this track's README documents, not a code
effect. Median per-round ratio 1.0405. Decode unchanged: tg64 24.27/24.19
vs 24.27/24.31 (expert decode is mmvq, the cap touches MMQ only). Gate ppl
-c 512 --chunks 8: 4.7508 cap on, 4.7508 cap off, both equal to the stock
gate value on this box. Greedy identity byte-exact on vs off, verified via
llama-server (790 bytes, non-emptiness asserted). NOTE: an earlier
llama-cli-based check of this patch was a FALSE PASS - redirected
llama-cli stdout produced two empty files that compared equal; see the
track README and the empty-file-greedy-identity-false-pass finding. Whole
series (0001+0002) apply-tested clean on pristine b10237.
---
diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/mmq.cuh b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/mmq.cuh
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/mmq.cuh
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/mmq.cuh
@@ -1493,6 +1493,32 @@
         }
     }

+    // MoE (mul_mat_id) on sm_121: J is otherwise chosen from ncols_max, the
+    // worst-case column count of a single expert segment (= n_tokens for
+    // mul_mat_id), which lands at J=128 for prefill batches. The GB10
+    // tensor-core MMQ measures fastest for the 256-expert top-8 segment mix
+    // at J=56-64 (target sweep on the qwen3.6 twin, which shares the expert
+    // shapes and top-8-of-256 routing; see that track series 0016). The
+    // RDNA4-style near-segment-width cap (J=32 there) LOSES here - the
+    // tensor cores amortize per-tile fixed cost over wide tiles, so the
+    // sweet spot between that and masked-padding waste sits at 64, not at
+    // the segment width. GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_MMQ_MOE_J restores stock.
+    if (args.ids_dst != nullptr && args.nchannels_y > 1 && J_best > 64 &&
+        GGML_CUDA_CC_IS_NVIDIA(cc) && ggml_cuda_highest_compiled_arch(cc) == 1210 &&
+        getenv("GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_MMQ_MOE_J") == nullptr) {
+        for (int J = 64; J < J_best; J += 8) {
+            const ggml_cuda_mmq_config config = ggml_cuda_mmq_get_config(type, J, fallback, cc);
+            if (config.type == GGML_TYPE_COUNT) {
+                continue;
+            }
+            if (mmq_get_nbytes_shared(config, cc) > smpbo) {
+                continue;
+            }
+            J_best = J;
+            break;
+        }
+    }
+
     switch (J_best) {
         case   8:
             launch_mul_mat_q<type,   8, fallback>(ctx, args, stream);
